; A call to a polymorphic function answers the type it was APPLIED at.
;
; `checkApp` returned the arrow's result position unsubstituted, so
; `(-> a a)` instantiated to `(-> _t5 _t5)` and the call answered
; `_t5` - a minted variable `tyCompat` matches against everything:
;
;   (:: idf (-> a a))  (fn (idf x) x)
;   (strLen (idf 7))     ->  check OK, exit 139
;   (strLen 7)           ->  AX3004, correctly
;
; The same expression, one call apart, with opposite answers. And
; everything downstream inherited it - a `match` arm whose type arrived
; through a polymorphic call was never joined, and the fall-through
; backstop's `Bool` exemption was reachable by laundering the scrutinee
; through `idf`.
;
; THIS IS THE SECOND ATTEMPT. The first (5f2a616) bound by OVERWRITING
; the instantiated type node, on the argument that `tyInstVar` mints
; one node per (call site, variable) so nobody else owns it. Two things
; were wrong and both were measured:
;
;   the type checker is not the only reader. `evStampFill` classifies
;   each argument against the same nodes to compute the ARC evidence
;   word, so a resolved parameter made `evMeet` miss a disagreement and
;   a callee retained a raw integer as a heap pointer - `check` OK,
;   segfault where the program had answered 32;
;
;   it bound BACKWARDS too, into the argument's node - and a local's
;   type lives in ONE scope entry that every reference answers with, so
;   the first callee a value met monomorphised it forever. 32 correct
;   programs stopped compiling.
;
; What is here instead: no mutation (`tyMatchInto` collects into a local
; env, `tySubstEnv` answers a COPY), no backward binding, and
; resolution ONLY at the spine root, where the value is a value and not
; a callee so nothing walks it again. That last condition is what keeps
; the evidence word pristine - resolving an intermediate result is
; visible to the NEXT level's `evStampFill` whether or not a node was
; mutated, which the non-mutating rewrite still got wrong until it was
; measured.
;
; THE NULL HANDLE is the other half, and it is the handle convention
; stated as one rule the checker knows. `0` is this language's null and
; inhabits every reference type - it is the default argument of every
; accessor in the tree - so a `0` passed at a type variable must not
; decide what that variable was instantiated at. Without that rule,
; `(strLen (mapGet m 7 0))` is AX3004 on correct code; 15 of the 18
; remaining false positives were exactly this shape and nothing else.
;
; Cost, measured against 116 programs that compile AND run correctly on
; the compiler before this: 3 refusals, all defensible - two effect
; operations declared `(-> String Int)` while answering a Str handle
; (the same imprecise declaration `sevSigil`, `importHelpText`,
; `BEnt.name` and `bsWith` each had), and one signature claiming a
; polymorphism its `strConcat` body does not have.
;
; STILL OPEN, deliberately: a LAMBDA's parameter type is not learned
; from its body, because that needs the backward direction that
; monomorphises locals. `(ap (lambda (s) (strLen s)) 7)` at
; `(-> (-> a Int) a Int)` still checks clean and still crashes. Closing
; it needs the handle convention decided rather than encoded one rule
; at a time - see the `tyReprClash` comment.

(import Str)

(:: idf (-> a a))
(fn (idf x) x)

; Refused: `idf` was applied at `Int`, so it answers `Int`.
(:: viaCall (-> Int Int))
(fn (viaCall n) (strLen (idf n)))

; Accepted, and the point of the null rule: the `0` default does not
; get to decide what `pick` was instantiated at.
(:: pick (-> Bool a a a))
(fn (pick b x y) (if b x y))

(:: viaNull (-> Bool String))
(fn (viaNull b) (pick b "hello" 0))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) (strLen (viaNull true)))
